# "Who's" (who is) for "whose" (belonging to whom). Common-error lists;
# examples are ours.
# why: Who's means "who is" or "who has". Whose asks or tells who something belongs to: whose turn, whose name.
expr owned [name, names, turn, fault, idea, job, car, house, phone, responsibility, book, money, team, dog, kid, kids, child, children, mother, father, parents, number, account, birthday, bag, keys, side, work, coat, seat, office, desk, email, laptop, project, decision]
expr main <(who's @owned), (who's)>

let message "Did you mean `whose` (belonging to whom)?"
let description "Fixes `who's turn` → `whose turn`."
let kind "Grammar"
let becomes "whose"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "Who's turn is it to cook?" "Whose turn is it to cook?"
test "The officer, who's name was not released, spoke briefly." "The officer, whose name was not released, spoke briefly."

allows "Whose turn is it to cook?"
allows "Who's coming tonight?"
allows "Who's been using my laptop?"
